course: dictionary
lesson: 2651
title: Strong words for strong feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are stronger than others. «Nasty» and «horrible» are fine for everyday things, but detestable is a strong, rather formal word that means you hate something very much. Context shows you its strength: when a character calls something detestable, the speaker is very angry or disgusted.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «Just let us once get past the stoats, with those detestable guns of theirs, and I assure you we shan't want any swords or pistols.» Badger is talking about the stoats' guns and says his side will not need swords or pistols. So detestable tells us he thinks the guns are wicked and hateful. Try swapping in «horrible» and the sentence still works, but detestable shows how strongly he feels.
explain: The word can describe a thing, a deed, a habit or a person. You might read about «detestable behaviour» or «a detestable man». In each case the word tells you that people strongly dislike it, because it is so cruel or mean that others cannot stand it.
fact: Detestable means very hateful, so bad or nasty that people strongly dislike it.
fact: Detestable can also describe a person who behaves so cruelly or meanly that others cannot stand them.
fact: Detestable is stronger than «nasty» or «horrible», and it shows deep disgust.
fact: Detestable is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in «detestable guns» or «a detestable habit».
fact: Detestable is a formal word, so you will often find it in older books and in angry speeches.
exercise: «...with those detestable guns of theirs...» What does detestable mean here? | Small and light | *Very hateful and nasty | Quiet and gentle | Shiny and new
exercise: Badger calls the stoats' guns detestable. What does he feel about them? | *He strongly hates them | He is puzzled by them | He is pleased with them | He is bored by them
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to detestable? | sleepy | cheerful | *hateful | tiny
test: «He told a detestable lie to get out of trouble.» What does detestable tell us about the lie? | It was small and funny | It was true and kind | *It was very hateful and wicked | It was whispered softly
test: Which phrase best fits the meaning of detestable? | Covered in mud and leaves | Always hungry and tired | *So cruel that others cannot stand him | Very tall and thin
exam: Why is detestable a stronger word than nasty? | It is only used for weather | It means exactly the same, so there is no difference | It is a shorter word | *It shows deeper hatred and disgust
